  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 AM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nEarly this morning, there are some chances for fog over the area. \nLight winds and nearly full humidity recovery over the entire \nforecast area will provide prime conditions for potentially \nwidespread development early this morning. Cloud cover, particularly \nover parts of northeastern South Dakota and western Minnesota, may \nlimit some of the radiational cooling this morning. However, surface \nobservations in that area indicated dew point depressions of 3 \ndegrees or less as of 06Z, so cloud cover may not pose much of a \nhindrance to fog development after all. Fog that does develop may \nrestrict visibility to a mile or lower. \n\nWith the onset of a heavy rainfall event nearly upon us, models \nstill appear to be coming a bit more into consensus on the location \nof the axis of heaviest rainfall. The NBM in particular had pushed \nnorthward over the past 24 hours, until the latest run (01Z) has it \nshifting back to the south. The center of the axis now passes just \nsouth of the Watertown area and stretches west to be just south of \nPierre as well. Euro ensemble guidance is in a decent level of \nagreement with this solution, as are many of the high-resolution \nmodels that are able to capture the full event at this point. As for \nrainfall totals, those have trended down over the past 24 hours as \nwell. There is still around an inch of spread within ensembles, but \nin general median values sit between 1.5\"-2.25\" over central and \neastern South Dakota. Probability to see 1\" or more sits at around \n70-90% over those areas, while probability to see 2\" or more has \nfallen to 40-60%. At this point, expectations are still that the \nheaviest rainfall will occur overnight Thursday into Friday morning. \n\nDespite the downward trend in rainfall totals, there is still a \nflooding threat, particularly with the convective element to some of \nthe storms. NBM 90th percentiles, a proxy for a \"reasonable worst \ncase scenario\" for an extended period of heavy rain, sit at around \n3\"-3.5\". Should this level of rain be realized, it would very likely \nexceed flash flood guidance. Antecedent conditions over eastern and \ncentral South Dakota will vary somewhat, but after the recent \nrainfall yesterday, much of the area sits at roughly 10-30% soil \nmoisture. When looking at model soundings, many of the \nmeteorological ingredients for flooding will be in place. Namely, a \nfully saturated profile with PWATs well above the 90th percentile \n(widespread PWATs around 1.75\" for this event) prove favorable, as \ndoes the deep warm cloud layer of 11-12k feet. There remain some \nquestion as to how quickly storms will be, as models give anywhere \nfrom 10-20 knots (which is a somewhat large variance when \nconsidering flooding potential). There is also the question of \ninstability. The best instability will be over central South Dakota, \nup to 1000 J/kg at times. Therefore, that is the area of highest \nconcern when it comes to flooding with this event, despite not \nstrictly being the highest level of QPF in the forecast area. \nParticularly if storm motion is slow, central South Dakota has the \nbest potential to see their rain come down over a shorter duration, \nas opposed to eastern South Dakota where rain will linger for \nlonger, but likely with lighter rainfall rates. \n\nAfter this round of rain moves out, the upper-level low currently \nover the western CONUS will progress eastward. Additional rain \nshowers will be possible over the weekend as the low moves overhead. \nA cooler airmass will take hold of the region due to the pattern \nshift, subsequently bringing below normal temperatures (highs in the \n60s) through the weekend and into the start of next week. Models \ntrend towards a warming trend back to near-normal by mid-week next \nweek.",
